          %title Explicit mathematics
          %h1#explicit_mathematics Explicit mathematics
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#the_basic_theory_of_operations_and_numbers"} The basic theory of operations and numbers
              %li
                %a{:href => "#elementary_comprehension"} Elementary comprehension
              %li
                %a{:href => "#join_and_inductive_generation"} Join and inductive generation
              %li
                %a{:href => "#universes_in_explicit_mathematics"} Universes in explicit mathematics
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %strong Explicit mathematics
            is a programme initiated by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Solomon+Feferman"} Solomon Feferman
            which is related to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
          %p
            It is also a range of foundational logical systems based on the
            = succeed "." do
              %span.newWikiWord
                logic of partial terms
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/logic+of+partial+terms"} ?
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/combinatory+algebra"} combinatory algebra
            %em classes
            is added. Sometimes, the alternative names
            %em classifications
            or
            %em types
            are used.
          %p
            The classes of explicit mathematics are not like the classes of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            %em names
            that can be operated on to construct new ones.
          %p
            The systems come in flavors with either intuitionistic or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+logic"} classical logic
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universes"} universes
          %p
            The primary systems are called
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_0
            (the classical variant) and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi T
                    %mn 0
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_0^i
            (the intuitionistic variant).
          %p
            In the 1980s there was a proof assistant and program extraction tool called PX based on a version of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_0
            relative to the Lisp programming language.
          %h2#the_basic_theory_of_operations_and_numbers The basic theory of operations and numbers
          %p
            The first-order part of the systems treat an applicative universe in the sense of combinatory algebra. It includes constants
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{k}
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{s}
            (combinators),
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{p}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} p
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{p}_0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} p
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{p}_1
            (pairing and projection),
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} s
                      %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{s}_N
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} p
                    %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{p}_N
            (zero, successor, and predecessor), and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                    %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{d}_N
            (definition by cases on the natural numbers), and possibly further applicative constants.
          %p
            There is one binary function symbol
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⋅
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {\cdot}
            for application (usual just indicated by juxtaposition), one unary relation symbol
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \downarrow
            for being defined, one unary relation symbol
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{N}
            for natural numbers, as well the binary equality relation.
          %p
            In the logic of partial terms, we use the usual abbreviation
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi t
                    %mo ↔
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mrow
                      %mi s
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                    %mo ∧
                    %mrow
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                    %mo →
                    %mi s
                    %mo =
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s \simeq t \leftrightarrow ({s\downarrow} \wedge {t\downarrow} \to s=t)
          %ol
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} k
                    %mi a
                    %mi b
                    %mo =
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{k} a b = a
              ,
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} s
                    %mi a
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} s
                    %mi a
                    %mi b
                    %mi c
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi a
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi b
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{s} a b \downarrow \wedge \mathrm{s} a b c \simeq a c(b c)
              ,
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} p
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∧
                    %msub
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} p
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{p}_0(a,b)=a \wedge \mathrm{p}_1(a,b) = b
              ,
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi N
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} s
                      %mi N
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0\in N \wedge (\forall x\in N)(\mathrm{s}_N x \in N)
              ,
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} s
                      %mi N
                    %mi x
                    %mo ≠
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ∧
                    %msub
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} p
                      %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} s
                      %mi N
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\forall x\in N)(\mathrm{s}_N x \ne 0 \wedge \mathrm{p}_N(\mathrm{s}_N x)=x)
              ,
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ≠
                    %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} s
                      %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} p
                      %mi N
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\forall x\in N)(x\ne0 \to \mathrm{s}_N(\mathrm{p}_N x) = x)
              ,
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi N
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi b
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi N
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi a
                    %mo =
                    %mi b
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                      %mi N
                    %mi u
                    %mi v
                    %mi a
                    %mi b
                    %mo =
                    %mi u
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\in N \wedge b\in N \wedge a=b \to \mathrm{d}_N u v a b = u
              ,
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi N
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi b
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi N
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi a
                    %mo ≠
                    %mi b
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                      %mi N
                    %mi u
                    %mi v
                    %mi a
                    %mi b
                    %mo =
                    %mi v
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\in N \wedge b\in N \wedge a\ne b \to \mathrm{d}_N u v a b = v
              \.
          %p From the first two axioms we get lambda-definability and a fixed-point combinator.
          %p
            To these axioms we add the scheme of induction over the natural numbers to obtain the theory
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi BON
                    %mo +
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} L
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %msub
                      %mi Ind
                      %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} BON + (\mathrm{L}{-}Ind_N)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi PA
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} PA
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi BON
                  %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} L
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %msub
                    %mi Ind
                    %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} BON + (\mathrm{L}{-}Ind_N)
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi PA
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} PA
            by coding operations as indices for recursive functions using Kleene’s
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            predicates.
          %h2#elementary_comprehension Elementary comprehension
          %p
            When we go from the purely applicative theory to the theories of classes and names, we add a second sort (for classes) as well as several new constants, a binary relation symbol
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∈
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \in
            (for membership) and a binary relation symbol for names
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℜ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Re
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℜ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi s
                  %mo ,
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Re(s, U)
            means that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
            is a name for the class
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℜ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mo ↔
                    %mo ∃
                    %mi U
                    %mi ℜ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi s
                    %mo ,
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Re(s) :\leftrightarrow \exists U \Re(s,U)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
            is a name.
          %p
            The basic axioms regarding classes and names state that every class has a name, that there are no homonyms, and that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℜ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Re
            respects extensional equality.
          %p
            The names of classes corresponding to elementary comprehension are generated from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi nat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} nat
            (natural numbers) and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi id
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} id
            (the equality relation) using
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi co
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} co
            (complements),
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi int
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} int
            (intersections),
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi dom
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} dom
            (domains), and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi inv
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} inv
            (inverse images). [These are for the classical systems. The intuitionistic systems are slightly different. Todo: look this up]
          %p
            Using the abbreviation
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                    %mover
                      %mo ∈
                      %mo ˙
                    %mi t
                    %mo :
                    %mo ↔
                    %mo ∃
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℜ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo ,
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi s
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s\dot\in t :\leftrightarrow \exists U(\Re(t,U) \wedge s\in U)
          %ol
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℜ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi nat
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mover
                      %mo ∈
                      %mo ˙
                    %mi nat
                    %mo ↔
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Re(nat) \wedge \forall x(x \dot\in nat \leftrightarrow N(x))
              ,
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℜ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi id
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mover
                      %mo ∈
                      %mo ˙
                    %mi id
                    %mo ↔
                    %mo ∃
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Re(id) \wedge \forall x(x \dot\in id \leftrightarrow \exists y(x = (y,y)))
              ,
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℜ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℜ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi co
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mover
                      %mo ∈
                      %mo ˙
                    %mi co
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↔
                    %mo ¬
                    %mi x
                    %mover
                      %mo ∈
                      %mo ˙
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Re(a) \to \Re(co(a)) \wedge \forall x(x \dot\in co(a) \leftrightarrow \neg x\dot\in a)
              ,
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℜ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi ℜ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℜ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi int
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mover
                      %mo ∈
                      %mo ˙
                    %mi int
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↔
                    %mi x
                    %mover
                      %mo ∈
                      %mo ˙
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi x
                    %mover
                      %mo ∈
                      %mo ˙
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Re(a) \wedge \Re(b) \to \Re(int(a,b)) \wedge \forall x(x \dot\in int(a,b) \leftrightarrow x \dot\in a \wedge x \dot\in b)
              ,
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℜ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℜ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi dom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mover
                      %mo ∈
                      %mo ˙
                    %mi dom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↔
                    %mo ∃
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo ∈
                      %mo ˙
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Re(a) \to \Re(dom(a)) \wedge \forall x(x \dot\in dom(a) \leftrightarrow \exists y((x,y) \dot\in a))
              ,
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℜ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℜ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi inv
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mover
                      %mo ∈
                      %mo ˙
                    %mi inv
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↔
                    %mi f
                    %mi x
                    %mover
                      %mo ∈
                      %mo ˙
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Re(a) \to \Re(inv(a,f)) \wedge \forall x(x \dot\in inv(a,f) \leftrightarrow f x \dot\in a)
              \.
          %p
            Again we add some induction over the natural numbers to obtain theories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi EC
                  %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} C
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %msub
                    %mi Ind
                    %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} EC + (\mathrm{C}{-}Ind_N)
            (induction axiom for classes) and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi EC
                  %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} L
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %msub
                    %mi Ind
                    %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} EC + (\mathrm{L}{-}Ind_N)
            (induction schema for all formulas). These correspond proof-theoretically to the subsystems of second-order arithmetic
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ACA
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ACA_0
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ACA
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ACA
          %h2#join_and_inductive_generation Join and inductive generation
          %p
            The
            %em join
            of classes gives disjoint unions of classes, and thus corresponds to the
            = succeed "-types" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
          %p [todo: the axioms for join and inductive generation.]
          %p
            The theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_0
            has elementary comprehension, join, inductive generation, and full induction over the natural numbers. It has the same proof-theoretic strength as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CZF"} CZF
            plus
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi REA
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} REA
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+extension+axiom"} regular extension axiom
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinal+analysis"} ordinal analysis
          %h2#universes_in_explicit_mathematics Universes in explicit mathematics
          %p [todo]
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "https://math.stanford.edu/~feferman/papers/DraftIntroFEM.pdf"} Introduction (draft)
            %li
              %p
                Solomon Feferman,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://home.inf.unibe.ch/~ltg/em_bibliography/feferman13.pdf"} How a Little Bit goes a Long Way: Predicative Foundations of Analysis
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "https://web.archive.org/web/20190108170710/http://www.ltg.unibe.ch/research/Foundations%20of%20Explicit%20Mathematics"} Foundations of explicit mathematics
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "http://home.inf.unibe.ch/~ltg/em_bibliography/"} Bibliography of explicit mathematics
            %li
              %p
                Reinhard Kahle and Anton Setzer,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.cs.swan.ac.uk/~csetzer/articles/kahleSetzerExtendedPredicativeMahloPohlersFestschrift.pdf"} An extended predicative definition of the Mahlo universe
            %li
              %p
                Susumu Hayashi and Hiroshi Nakano,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://dl.acm.org/citation.cfm?id=62010"} PX: a computational logic
